WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.803
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:08.108 --> 00:00:11.644
shinytest2 is a
wonderful package

00:00:11.644 --> 00:00:15.715
to help do regression testing
for your Shiny applications.

00:00:15.715 --> 00:00:17.450
And we're going to
perform this regression

00:00:17.450 --> 00:00:21.087
testing inside testthat.

00:00:21.087 --> 00:00:24.691
And it's not regression testing
like linear model or something

00:00:24.691 --> 00:00:25.191
like that.

00:00:25.191 --> 00:00:28.695
This is a consistent
behavior over time,

00:00:28.695 --> 00:00:30.830
and so we do not
want it to regress,

00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:32.365
or we do not want it to change.

00:00:32.365 --> 00:00:34.100
That's what I mean for
regression testing.

00:00:38.338 --> 00:00:40.840
Before we get into
shinytest2 and how it works,

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:43.643
I just want to do this demo app.

00:00:43.643 --> 00:00:49.349
This demo app is short,
a little toy example.

00:00:49.349 --> 00:00:52.585
We have a fluid page
that has a text input.

00:00:52.585 --> 00:00:54.187
It's like what's your name.

00:00:54.187 --> 00:00:55.822
We can fill it out.

00:00:55.822 --> 00:00:58.058
And then we also
have an action button

00:00:58.058 --> 00:01:03.196
of greet, the text greet.

00:01:03.196 --> 00:01:05.632
And then we have
two text outputs.

00:01:05.632 --> 00:01:07.500
One is a greeting,
like "Hello, Barrett!"

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:09.669
And then the other
one is what is

00:01:09.669 --> 00:01:11.571
the first letter in your name.

00:01:11.571 --> 00:01:15.542
And it's neat, like
it's short little app.

00:01:15.542 --> 00:01:17.811
So if I have it on
here, I have "Barrett."

00:01:17.811 --> 00:01:18.478
I click Greet.

00:01:18.478 --> 00:01:20.080
We get, "Hello Barrett!"

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:24.417
And if I'm here, we could
do like "Shinytest2."

00:01:24.417 --> 00:01:25.218
"What is your name?

00:01:25.218 --> 00:01:26.152
Shinytest2."

00:01:26.152 --> 00:01:27.654
Greet, "Hello Shinytest2."

00:01:27.654 --> 00:01:31.591
The first letter
is S. So it works.

00:01:31.591 --> 00:01:33.359
It's a nice little app.

00:01:33.359 --> 00:01:34.961
The app for the server--

00:01:34.961 --> 00:01:38.698
what it does is we have a render
text that requires the name

00:01:38.698 --> 00:01:44.637
and given a Greet click,
we will say, "Hello" name.

00:01:44.637 --> 00:01:47.674
And then for the first letter
calculation, we require a name,

00:01:47.674 --> 00:01:51.511
and we extract the first
character of the name.

00:01:51.511 --> 00:01:53.813
And we send it tolower.

00:01:53.813 --> 00:01:56.249
And we will bind all
of that calculations

00:01:56.249 --> 00:01:59.152
to when the Greet
button is clicked.

00:01:59.152 --> 00:02:01.321
Given this first letter,
whenever it updates,

00:02:01.321 --> 00:02:03.656
we will then call
render text and say

00:02:03.656 --> 00:02:07.260
the first letter in your
name is this first letter.

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:09.162
Awesome.

00:02:09.162 --> 00:02:11.698
So let's imagine that I have--

00:02:11.698 --> 00:02:15.168
well I have now created this
app, or I am working on a team,

00:02:15.168 --> 00:02:18.838
and I want to make sure that
behaviors are consistent.

00:02:18.838 --> 00:02:22.442
And tests are worth so much,
because you don't necessarily

00:02:22.442 --> 00:02:25.011
need to communicate that
because the test is there.

00:02:25.011 --> 00:02:27.313
And if the test breaks,
then we can figure out

00:02:27.313 --> 00:02:29.048
what's happening.

00:02:29.048 --> 00:02:32.285
And this also allows you to do
other things like sweeping code

00:02:32.285 --> 00:02:33.052
changes.

00:02:33.052 --> 00:02:35.722
Like what if I want to
change the GUTs of how

00:02:35.722 --> 00:02:37.290
first letter is calculated.

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:40.460
I know this is a
small function, but it

00:02:40.460 --> 00:02:42.195
could be much more complicated.

00:02:42.195 --> 00:02:45.899
And having tests allow you
to do these sweeping changes

00:02:45.899 --> 00:02:48.568
with confidence,
otherwise you just

00:02:48.568 --> 00:02:50.537
look out and go, no,
that's close enough.

00:02:50.537 --> 00:02:51.070
I don't know.

00:02:51.070 --> 00:02:52.872
I hope I covered everything.

00:02:52.872 --> 00:02:55.275
At least with tests, we can
say all of the tests pass.

00:02:55.275 --> 00:02:56.743
And if you're
missing something, we

00:02:56.743 --> 00:02:59.512
can add them in and then
make your test more robust.

00:02:59.512 --> 00:03:01.915
When you run record
test, it opens up

00:03:01.915 --> 00:03:03.850
your application in
the Chrome browser

00:03:03.850 --> 00:03:05.285
or Chromium based browser.

00:03:05.285 --> 00:03:07.887
Mine by default
is set to Chrome.

00:03:07.887 --> 00:03:10.356
It'll have your
app in this IFrame.

00:03:10.356 --> 00:03:13.293
And on the sidebar is
actually the recorder.

00:03:13.293 --> 00:03:15.862
And we can see
there's some buttons

00:03:15.862 --> 00:03:19.199
to call Expect Shiny values
or Expect screenshot.

00:03:19.199 --> 00:03:22.335
And then there's also
an area of all the code

00:03:22.335 --> 00:03:25.104
that will be executed
later that is

00:03:25.104 --> 00:03:28.007
able to replay your recording.

00:03:28.007 --> 00:03:29.409
So let's make a recording.

00:03:29.409 --> 00:03:30.577
This is "What is your name?"

00:03:30.577 --> 00:03:32.712
My name is Barrett.

00:03:32.712 --> 00:03:33.880
So let's Greet Barrett.

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:35.081
And it'll say, "Hello Barret!

00:03:35.081 --> 00:03:37.850
The first letter
in your name is b!"

00:03:37.850 --> 00:03:40.887
And we can see that I set
inputs, name is Barrett.

00:03:40.887 --> 00:03:43.356
And then I clicked
the Greet button,

00:03:43.356 --> 00:03:46.125
and then an output
value is updated.

00:03:46.125 --> 00:03:48.161
Awesome.

00:03:48.161 --> 00:03:52.465
So I can test this or save
this as "hello-barrett,"

00:03:52.465 --> 00:03:57.270
because that is the name that I
used and just for my reference.

00:03:57.270 --> 00:04:00.907
And I'll click-- oh, but
I need to make sure first

00:04:00.907 --> 00:04:03.509
before-- because I
can't save right away.

00:04:03.509 --> 00:04:04.477
Well, let me.

00:04:04.477 --> 00:04:07.180
So I need to say
Expect Shiny values.

00:04:07.180 --> 00:04:10.316
And this will keep track--

00:04:10.316 --> 00:04:13.319
the expect values will keep
track of all your input,

00:04:13.319 --> 00:04:15.455
output, and exported values.

00:04:15.455 --> 00:04:18.658
And exported values, we'll
get to in another video.

00:04:18.658 --> 00:04:20.893
So once we have at
least one expectation,

00:04:20.893 --> 00:04:25.098
we can then click
Save test and exit.

00:04:25.098 --> 00:04:29.269
This will save the test and
then immediately run the test--

00:04:29.269 --> 00:04:31.037
play back the test again.

00:04:31.037 --> 00:04:33.172
And there are some
warnings, because we

00:04:33.172 --> 00:04:34.340
have some new snapshots.

00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:35.341
But that's OK.

00:04:35.341 --> 00:04:37.877
And it looks like it all
passed, because there's also

00:04:37.877 --> 00:04:42.548
nothing to say that the
snapshots would fail.

00:04:42.548 --> 00:04:46.653
So let's take a look
at what was created.

00:04:46.653 --> 00:04:49.188
So we have our
app that are here.

00:04:49.188 --> 00:04:50.423
Awesome.

00:04:50.423 --> 00:04:53.593
And then the tests folder,
it will be a sibling to that.

00:04:53.593 --> 00:04:57.630
And in there, we will have a
testthat.R which internally,

00:04:57.630 --> 00:05:00.933
it just has shinytest2 test app.

00:05:00.933 --> 00:05:05.238
And then next to the testthat.R
is a testthat folder.

00:05:05.238 --> 00:05:07.573
There there's three parts here.

00:05:07.573 --> 00:05:09.809
The first part is
the snaps folder.

00:05:09.809 --> 00:05:12.779
We'll go inside
that in a second.

00:05:12.779 --> 00:05:17.950
The second one is the setup.R.
setup.R is there for you

00:05:17.950 --> 00:05:19.786
when your little more
complicated apps where

00:05:19.786 --> 00:05:22.789
you have an R folder or you
have global.R your Shiny

00:05:22.789 --> 00:05:23.723
application.

00:05:23.723 --> 00:05:27.193
We load all of those
information inside setup.R,

00:05:27.193 --> 00:05:31.764
so that your tests have
that information available.

00:05:31.764 --> 00:05:34.600
And the last one
is test-shinytest2.

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:36.903
This is the default file
name for recordings.

00:05:36.903 --> 00:05:39.272
And it'll say
shinytest2 recording--

00:05:39.272 --> 00:05:41.808
whatever name you gave.

00:05:41.808 --> 00:05:45.244
So if we remember from the
recording that we made,

00:05:45.244 --> 00:05:47.180
I created a new app.

00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:50.683
It gives the same height and
width of the recording window.

00:05:50.683 --> 00:05:53.152
That way when it replays,
it's under the same height

00:05:53.152 --> 00:05:54.520
and width.

00:05:54.520 --> 00:05:56.622
We then say app$set_inputs.

00:05:56.622 --> 00:05:59.425
I typed in "Barrett"
for the name.

00:05:59.425 --> 00:06:01.861
I then clicked the Greet
button, and then we called

00:06:01.861 --> 00:06:03.930
app$expect_values.

00:06:03.930 --> 00:06:07.333
Expect values is actually
a wrapper around shiny test

00:06:07.333 --> 00:06:10.603
or actually a wrapper
around teststhat, expect,

00:06:10.603 --> 00:06:12.472
snapshot file.

00:06:12.472 --> 00:06:15.441
And this file will contain
a JSON representation

00:06:15.441 --> 00:06:19.612
of your input, output,
and export values.

00:06:19.612 --> 00:06:22.448
And that is stored
in the _snaps folder.

00:06:22.448 --> 00:06:24.484
So we will open up _snaps.

00:06:24.484 --> 00:06:26.419
We'll open up
shinytest2, because that

00:06:26.419 --> 00:06:28.788
is the name of the
file, shinytest2.

00:06:28.788 --> 00:06:31.057
So that name.

00:06:31.057 --> 00:06:34.660
And then there was the first
JSON file that was created.

00:06:34.660 --> 00:06:40.733
And this one if we click it, has
my input, output, and export.

00:06:40.733 --> 00:06:43.703
So my inputs is at
the time of calling

00:06:43.703 --> 00:06:46.606
expect values, which was
after setting an input

00:06:46.606 --> 00:06:49.842
and after clicking Greet button.

00:06:49.842 --> 00:06:53.312
We have input of "greet": 1 for
one time to click the button.

00:06:53.312 --> 00:06:55.348
And name was Barrett.

00:06:55.348 --> 00:06:57.683
The output was
"first_letter" was,

00:06:57.683 --> 00:06:59.485
"The first letter
your name is b!"

00:06:59.485 --> 00:07:01.988
And the greeting
is, "Hello Barrett!"

00:07:01.988 --> 00:07:04.290
We didn't export
any test values yet.

00:07:04.290 --> 00:07:06.459
But we'll get into
that in a later video.

00:07:06.459 --> 00:07:07.427
So this is our test.

00:07:07.427 --> 00:07:08.961
This is awesome.

00:07:08.961 --> 00:07:12.031
We can, if we want, change
the height and width.

00:07:12.031 --> 00:07:14.534
We can add more tests.

00:07:14.534 --> 00:07:18.171
This is our test
file to work with.

00:07:18.171 --> 00:07:19.672
And as a side note,
you might notice

00:07:19.672 --> 00:07:24.010
that there's a PNG file here
created next to the JSON file.

00:07:24.010 --> 00:07:28.915
This is there, because images
typically are fairly brittle.

00:07:28.915 --> 00:07:32.552
And we don't like to use
them if we can help it,

00:07:32.552 --> 00:07:35.021
because there's lots of
things outside of our control

00:07:35.021 --> 00:07:40.026
that we can't account for, such
as if the R version updates,

00:07:40.026 --> 00:07:43.529
or let's say you change
your version of DT,

00:07:43.529 --> 00:07:48.100
or let's say you update
your system font.

00:07:48.100 --> 00:07:48.701
Yeah.

00:07:48.701 --> 00:07:50.102
There's many
different things that

00:07:50.102 --> 00:07:55.641
are outside of Shiny, outside of
R that are not in your control.

00:07:55.641 --> 00:07:59.111
And screenshots will
fail if this happens.

00:07:59.111 --> 00:08:03.082
And no one likes to see
that, but that'll happen.

00:08:03.082 --> 00:08:06.586
So if you can, try to do
everything as much as possible

00:08:06.586 --> 00:08:11.624
using your JSON or your
values, and try to just test

00:08:11.624 --> 00:08:12.992
the things that you own.

00:08:12.992 --> 00:08:15.428
Unless you're a package
developer, then by all means,

00:08:15.428 --> 00:08:18.464
we need to start taking
pictures and making sure things

00:08:18.464 --> 00:08:19.632
render properly.

00:08:19.632 --> 00:08:21.701
But otherwise, we can
give that responsibility

00:08:21.701 --> 00:08:26.706
back to the app developers
not us as app developers.

00:08:26.706 --> 00:08:30.643
But the image is there
to take us a snapshot

00:08:30.643 --> 00:08:32.945
to let us know what
the app look like when

00:08:32.945 --> 00:08:34.614
we called expect values.

00:08:34.614 --> 00:08:37.416
So let's do, hello-barrett-001.

00:08:37.416 --> 00:08:40.419
And this will open
up my application.

00:08:40.419 --> 00:08:43.055
And we can see that
Chromote believes

00:08:43.055 --> 00:08:46.692
the app looked like this where
it says, "What is your name?"

00:08:46.692 --> 00:08:47.193
"Barret."

00:08:47.193 --> 00:08:47.693
Greet.

00:08:47.693 --> 00:08:48.327
"Hello Barret!"

00:08:48.327 --> 00:08:50.429
Your first letter name is b.

00:08:50.429 --> 00:08:51.330
Awesome.

00:08:51.330 --> 00:08:52.632
That worked out great.

00:08:52.632 --> 00:08:54.667
So one of the things that
we can do interactively

00:08:54.667 --> 00:08:58.037
is we can take our app, and
we can run it in the console

00:08:58.037 --> 00:09:02.508
as long as we're sitting in our
working directory of our Shiny

00:09:02.508 --> 00:09:03.643
application.

00:09:03.643 --> 00:09:06.379
This uses the app driver object.

00:09:06.379 --> 00:09:10.783
And this object is something
that drives shinytest2.

00:09:10.783 --> 00:09:13.786
And it runs your Shiny
application in the background.

00:09:13.786 --> 00:09:16.322
And it also does your
communication with Chromote.

00:09:16.322 --> 00:09:17.957
And so it handles all of that.

00:09:17.957 --> 00:09:19.926
And we can set our inputs.

00:09:19.926 --> 00:09:23.996
We can also retrieve inputs, and
outputs, or different values,

00:09:23.996 --> 00:09:27.066
and click many different things.

00:09:27.066 --> 00:09:29.936
So if we do this, one of the
things that I really enjoy is

00:09:29.936 --> 00:09:35.374
that we can call app$view, and
view will open up a web page

00:09:35.374 --> 00:09:38.778
of our headless Chrome browser.

00:09:38.778 --> 00:09:41.647
And I think this is really,
really neat, because now it's

00:09:41.647 --> 00:09:46.252
not a black box as
to what is happening.

00:09:46.252 --> 00:09:47.620
So now I have my app.

00:09:47.620 --> 00:09:53.092
And this is what Chromote
is representing as-- what

00:09:53.092 --> 00:09:55.394
is happening within the app.

00:09:55.394 --> 00:09:58.631
So if we actually just
step through this code

00:09:58.631 --> 00:10:02.468
when in time, we can actually
see the app update to the left.

00:10:02.468 --> 00:10:06.439
So we can call
set_inputs(name="Barret").

00:10:06.439 --> 00:10:08.040
Click Greet.

00:10:08.040 --> 00:10:10.810
And then if I wanted to, I
could call expect_values,

00:10:10.810 --> 00:10:13.679
or I could even
say app$get_values.

00:10:18.084 --> 00:10:20.252
And this will return
all the values.

00:10:20.252 --> 00:10:23.155
Or if I wanted to do something
like just a particular one,

00:10:23.155 --> 00:10:26.325
I could say app$get
values(output = "greeting").

00:10:28.861 --> 00:10:31.864
And it's just that
one particular value.

00:10:31.864 --> 00:10:36.469
Or since if it's just your one
value, you can say get value

00:10:36.469 --> 00:10:41.073
and it'll turn back the regular
result. So it's very exciting.

00:10:41.073 --> 00:10:45.711
You can debug your headless
tiny application, and headless

00:10:45.711 --> 00:10:48.914
Chrome instance, and your
Shiny application all

00:10:48.914 --> 00:10:49.682
at the same time.

00:10:49.682 --> 00:10:50.883
Save the code that you want.

00:10:50.883 --> 00:10:52.952
And then we can replay
them afterwards.

00:10:52.952 --> 00:10:57.123
Or just going in the top
right and saying, run tests.

00:10:57.123 --> 00:10:59.892
You will load up.

00:10:59.892 --> 00:11:01.994
We'll get there and
eventually pass one

00:11:01.994 --> 00:11:04.463
for that expect_values, because
that's the only expectation

00:11:04.463 --> 00:11:05.631
in this whole test file.

00:11:05.631 --> 00:11:07.433
Test complete.

00:11:07.433 --> 00:11:07.933
Perfect.

00:11:07.933 --> 00:11:11.570
[MUSIC PLAYING]

